#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163227 :tutorial/content "Suppose we are given the quadratic form \n\n$$\nQ(\\mathbf{x})= x_1^2-8x_1x_2 + x_2^2\n$$\n\n and the matrix ![](images/13/13fad01fd9fcf327f8c45afa8264d92d9de1db135b7a87add4e43b9617574d8d.svg) that defines  the **change of variable**\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x} =P \\mathbf{y} ,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ![](images/c6/c6f0e1bf4282bf2ffedc462005ea30ad8bbe07917bab42eaf7fedf8fd2c49ef7.svg) and ![](images/ad/ad7dec0dac6e2a50d428c7d7dbeda4e71812df2f6ac2d80d6d834ffc82f4a0cb.svg) \n\n How do we express $Q(\\mathbf{x})$ in terms of the components of $\\mathbf{y}?$\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n![](images/ae/aefd4438342fcc5201fd2a3a04e4419f9c5189c4cd103eb63fa1ab6d464edc35.svg) \n\ngives the following expressions for $x_1$ and $x_2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =2{y}_{1}-{y}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} & =-{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, if we substitute these into our quadratic form expression, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & ={x}_{1}^{2}-8{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =(2{y}_{1}-{y}_{2})^{2}+8(2{y}_{1}-{y}_{2}){y}_{2}+(-{y}_{2})^{2} \\\\  & =4{y}_{1}^{2}-4{y}_{1}{y}_{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}+16{y}_{1}{y}_{2}-8{y}_{2}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =4{y}_{1}^{2}+12{y}_{1}{y}_{2}-6{y}_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "129771b6-7bea-5c9d-b4c7-05ef8a7493b2" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4885 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163236 :tutorial/content "Suppose we are given a quadratic form \n\n$$\nQ(\\mathbf{x})= \\mathbf{x}^T \\! A\\mathbf{x}\n$$\n\n \n\nand an invertible matrix $P$ that defines  the change of variable\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x} =P \\mathbf{y}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nIf this change of variable is applied to the quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf{x}),$ what will be the new matrix of $Q$?\n\nBy making the necessary substitutions, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & ={x}^{T}\\,Ax \\\\  & =(Py)^{T}\\,A\\,(Py) \\\\  & ={y}^{T}{P}^{T}\\,AP\\,y \\\\  & ={y}^{T}({P}^{T}AP)y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  the new matrix of our quadratic form is \n\n$$\nB=  P^T \\! A P.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "61f41743-ca11-557a-b0c1-bba72921428a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4901 :tutorial/title "The Connection Between Matrices of Quadratic Forms in Different Bases"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163237 :tutorial/content "Two quadratic forms $Q_1$ and $Q_2$ on $\\Bbb R^n$ are said to be **equivalent** if they can be obtained from each other through a change of variable.\n\n*The two quadratic forms with matrices $A$ and $B$ are equivalent if and only if there exists an invertible matrix $P$ such that* \n\n$$\nB = P^T \\! A \\, P.\n$$\n\n**Note:** Equivalent quadratic forms represent the same function from  $\\Bbb R^n$ to $\\Bbb R.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "abe7e02c-7997-5e41-9c51-98c6291198a1" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4902 :tutorial/title "Equivalent Quadratic Forms"}]}
